//? Title: Adler-32: Two Running Sums and What They Miss
//? Subtitle: Derivations, error-detection theorems and a collision model for zlib's checksum, with datamunge's algorithms::adler32
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Adler-32, the checksum in every zlib stream, keeps two running sums modulo
the prime \(p=65521\). We derive its closed form
\(B=n+\sum_i(n-i+1)x_i\) and the zlib constants. These include NMAX = 5552,
the longest run that can be summed before reducing, and the formulas that
combine two checksums or slide one along a window. The difference of two
messages' sums gives three detection results. Every change to one byte is
caught. A change to two bytes is missed only when the bytes are a multiple
of \(p\) apart. Some three-byte changes are always missed, such as "aca"
and "bab", which CRC-32 can never confuse. A normal approximation to the
two sums gives a collision model with three regimes. The probability falls
as \(n^{-2}\), then as \(n^{-1/2}\), and only after about 100 000 bytes
reaches the \(1/p^2\approx2^{-32}\) of an ideal 32-bit check. Two hundred
and sixty thousand random messages per length confirm the model. The 1797
handwritten digits from `dm::datasets::digits()` collide three times more
often than random data of the same spread. This is because Adler's two sums
are, in effect, the amount of ink and its vertical position.
)

\toc

> Two running sums

Start from \(A_0=1\) and \(B_0=0\). For each byte \(x_k\) of an
\(n\)-byte message, update both sums modulo the prime \(p=65521\):

$$
A_k = A_{k-1}+x_k, \qquad B_k = B_{k-1}+A_k \pmod p .
$$
\alttext(A sub k equals A sub k minus 1 plus x sub k, and B sub k equals B sub k minus 1 plus A sub k, modulo p.)

The checksum is \(2^{16}B_n+A_n\). Unrolling the recursion gives a closed form:

$$
A_n = 1+\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i, \qquad
B_n = \sum_{k=1}^{n} A_k = \sum_{k=1}^{n}\Bigl(1+\sum_{i\le k} x_i\Bigr) = n+\sum_{i=1}^{n}(n-i+1)\,x_i .
$$
\alttext(A sub n equals 1 plus the sum of the bytes. B sub n is the sum of the prefix values A sub k, which equals n plus the sum over i of n minus i plus 1 times x sub i.)

\(A\) is a plain byte total and cannot tell "ABCD" from "ABDC". \(B\)
weights each byte by the number of positions from it to the end, so
reordering changes \(B\).

The modulus is the largest prime below \(2^{16}\). Being prime matters for
the theorems below. It costs only 15 unused values per half: there are
\(p^2\) possible checksums, against \(2^{32}\) 32-bit values, and
\(p^2/2^{32}=0.99955\).

The table checks the library against reference values computed with Python's
`zlib.adler32`. It also checks the closed form directly, and includes the
first undetected change, which is explained below.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::print("| message | n | A | B | Adler-32 | closed-form B | CRC-32 |\n");
  std::print("| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for (std::string m : {"", "a", "abc", "Wikipedia", "ABCD", "ABDC", "aca", "bab"}) {
    const std::uint32_t sum = alg::adler32(m);
    std::uint64_t closed = m.size();
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < m.size(); ++i) closed += (m.size() - i) * static_cast<unsigned char>(m[i]);
    std::print("| \"{}\" | {} | {} | {} | 0x{:08X} | {} | 0x{:08X} |\n", m, m.size(), sum & 0xFFFF, sum >> 16,
               sum, closed % alg::adler32_modulus, alg::crc32(m));
  }
}
```
