\caption(Adler-32 of short messages, its two halves, the closed form for \(B\), and CRC-32 for comparison. "Wikipedia" gives the reference value 0x11E60398 and "abc" gives zlib's 0x024D0127)

> Speed: deferring the modulo

Reducing modulo \(p\) after every byte is the slow part. zlib instead adds
up runs of bytes in 32-bit integers and reduces once per run. How long can
a run be? Start a run with \(A,B\le p-1\) and feed it \(n\) bytes of 255.
After byte \(k\), \(A\le p-1+255k\). Summing these bounds gives

$$
B \;\le\; (p-1) + \sum_{k=1}^{n}\bigl(p-1+255k\bigr) \;=\; 255\,\frac{n(n+1)}{2}+(n+1)(p-1) \;\le\; 2^{32}-1 .
$$
\alttext(B is at most 255 times n times n plus 1 over 2, plus n plus 1 times p minus 1, which must not exceed 2 to the 32 minus 1.)

The largest \(n\) satisfying this is zlib's NMAX. `algorithms::adler32_update`
uses it, and its tests check it against byte-by-byte reduction on all-0xFF
input, the worst case.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  const std::uint64_t p = alg::adler32_modulus, limit = 0xFFFFFFFFULL;
  auto bound = [&](std::uint64_t n) { return 255 * n * (n + 1) / 2 + (n + 1) * (p - 1); };
  std::uint64_t n = 0;
  while (bound(n + 1) <= limit) ++n;
  std::print("| n | worst-case B | fits in 32 bits |\n| ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for (std::uint64_t k : {n - 1, n, n + 1}) std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", k, bound(k), bound(k) <= limit);
  std::print("\nLargest run: {}; library constant adler32_nmax = {}\n", n, alg::adler32_nmax);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | worst-case B | fits in 32 bits |
| ---: | ---: | :--- |
| 5551 | 4293208920 | true |
| 5552 | 4294690200 | true |
| 5553 | 4296171735 | false |

Largest run: 5552; library constant adler32_nmax = 5552
// result_end
\caption(The worst-case second sum around NMAX: 5552 bytes is the longest run that cannot overflow)

> Combining and rolling

Both sums are linear in the bytes, so Adler-32 is easy to compose. Let
\(M_1\) have sums \((A_1,B_1)\), and let \(M_2\), of length \(m\), have
\((A_2,B_2)\). In the concatenation \(M_1M_2\), the \(k\)-th prefix sum
inside \(M_2\) is \(A_1-1\) plus \(M_2\)'s own \(k\)-th prefix sum.
Summing over \(k\) gives

$$
A = A_1+A_2-1, \qquad B = B_1+B_2+m\,(A_1-1) \pmod p .
$$
\alttext(A equals A1 plus A2 minus 1, and B equals B1 plus B2 plus m times A1 minus 1, modulo p.)

This is `algorithms::adler32_combine`. With it, the checksums of
separately compressed pieces can be joined without rereading the data.

Sliding a window of \(w\) bytes one byte along drops \(x_{\text{out}}\),
whose weight was \(w\). It adds \(x_{\text{in}}\) with weight 1, and every
other byte's weight falls by one, which subtracts one copy of each
remaining byte. That gives

$$
A' = A-x_{\text{out}}+x_{\text{in}}, \qquad B' = B-w\,x_{\text{out}}+A'-1 \pmod p ,
$$
\alttext(A prime equals A minus x out plus x in, and B prime equals B minus w times x out plus A prime minus 1, modulo p.)

So each step takes constant time (`adler32_roll`, and `adler32_windows`
for every window). rsync relies on this.

The block below checks the three identities on a text stream: the 891
Titanic passenger names from `dm::datasets::titanic()`, one per line.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  const auto titanic = dm::datasets::titanic();
  std::string text;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < titanic.nrows(); ++r) text += titanic.string_at("Name", r) + "\n";

  int update_ok = 0, combine_ok = 0, cuts = 0;
  for (std::size_t cut = 0; cut <= text.size(); cut += 997, ++cuts) {
    const auto head = text.substr(0, cut), tail = text.substr(cut);
    update_ok += alg::adler32_update(alg::adler32(head), tail) == alg::adler32(text);
    combine_ok += alg::adler32_combine(alg::adler32(head), alg::adler32(tail), tail.size()) == alg::adler32(text);
  }
  const std::size_t w = 32;
  const auto windows = alg::adler32_windows(text, w);
  std::size_t roll_ok = 0;
  for (std::size_t s = 0; s < windows.size(); ++s) roll_ok += windows[s] == alg::adler32(text.substr(s, w));

  std::print("Stream: {} bytes, Adler-32 0x{:08X}\n\n", text.size(), alg::adler32(text));
  std::print("| identity | checks | agree |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| adler32_update(adler32(head), tail) | {} | {} |\n", cuts, update_ok);
  std::print("| adler32_combine(head, tail, length) | {} | {} |\n", cuts, combine_ok);
  std::print("| adler32_windows(text, 32) vs direct | {} | {} |\n", windows.size(), roll_ok);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Stream: 24917 bytes, Adler-32 0x46408291

| identity | checks | agree |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| adler32_update(adler32(head), tail) | 25 | 25 |
| adler32_combine(head, tail, length) | 25 | 25 |
| adler32_windows(text, 32) vs direct | 24886 | 24886 |
// result_end
\caption(The update, combine and rolling-window formulas agree with checksumming from scratch at every cut point and every window of the names stream)

The figure follows both sums along the start of that stream.
\(A_k\) climbs by about 86 per byte, the mean byte value of the names.
\(B_k\) grows quadratically and passes \(p\) after about
\(\sqrt{2p/86}\approx 39\) bytes. After that it wraps over and over. A
message of 30 bytes therefore uses only part of the range of \(B\), and
\(A\) does not reach \(p\) until byte 760. That is the source of
the weakness on short messages examined below.

```{cpp, results="output", file="figures/adler32_running_sums.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto titanic = dm::datasets::titanic();
  std::string text;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < titanic.nrows(); ++r) text += titanic.string_at("Name", r) + "\n";

  std::vector<double> k, a, b;
  std::uint32_t sum = 1;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 1200; ++i) {
    sum = alg::adler32_update(sum, text.substr(i, 1));
    k.push_back(i + 1.0);
    a.push_back(sum & 0xFFFF);
    b.push_back(sum >> 16);
  }
  const double p = alg::adler32_modulus;
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                 .lines(k, a, "A", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 1.5)
                 .lines({0.0, 1200.0}, {p, p}, "p = 65521", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.0)
                 .y_limits(0, 70000)
                 .title("First sum A_k")
                 .x_label("bytes k")
                 .y_label("value"));
  layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                 .lines(k, b, "B", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.0)
                 .x_limits(0, 300)
                 .y_limits(0, 70000)
                 .title("Second sum B_k (first 300 bytes)")
                 .x_label("bytes k")
                 .y_label("value"));
  layout.size(1100, 440).save_svg("build/adler32_running_sums.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/adler32_running_sums.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Running sums over the start of the Titanic names stream. A rises slowly and wraps once at byte 760; B wraps modulo p every few dozen bytes)
\alttext(Two line charts. Left: A sub k rises almost linearly from 1 to 65521 over the first 760 bytes, then wraps to zero and climbs again. Right: B sub k rises as a parabola and wraps around 65521 first after 38 bytes, then every 10 to 20 bytes.)

> What the checksum can and cannot detect

Corrupt a message by adding \(\delta_i\) to byte \(i\), with
\(|\delta_i|\le 255\). The closed form shows that the checksum changes
by

$$
\Delta A = \sum_i \delta_i, \qquad \Delta B = \sum_i (n-i+1)\,\delta_i \pmod p .
$$
\alttext(Delta A is the sum of the deltas, and Delta B is the sum of n minus i plus 1 times delta i, modulo p.)

An error is missed exactly when both changes vanish modulo \(p\). Three
results follow.

1. **One byte.** \(\Delta A=\delta_i\) with \(0<|\delta_i|<p\), so every
   single-byte error is detected.
2. **Two bytes** \(i<j\). From \(\Delta A\equiv0\), \(\delta_j=-\delta_i\),
   since \(|\delta_i+\delta_j|\le 510<p\). Then
   \(\Delta B=(j-i)\,\delta_i\). Since \(p\) is prime and does not divide
   \(\delta_i\), this vanishes only when \(p\mid j-i\). A two-byte error is
   missed only when the bytes are exactly 65521, 131042, … apart and
   change by opposite amounts. A modulus of \(2^{16}\) would be worse: it
   misses two-byte errors at distance \(2^{15}\) with \(\delta=\pm128\).
3. **Three adjacent bytes.** Take \(\delta=(e,-2e,e)\), a second
   difference. Then \(\Delta A=0\) and \(\Delta B=3e-4e+e=0\) exactly, for
   any message. "aca" and "bab" (\(e=1\)) are such a pair. CRC-32 cannot
   miss this, because its degree-32 generator polynomial detects every
   error confined to 32 consecutive bits.

For a *random* replacement of three bytes, the probability of landing on
this pattern follows from the distribution of the difference of two
uniform bytes, \(P(x-y=k)=(256-|k|)/256^2\):

$$
P_3 = \sum_{0<|e|\le127} \frac{(256-|e|)^2\,(256-2|e|)}{256^6} .
$$
\alttext(P sub 3 is the sum over nonzero e with absolute value at most 127 of 256 minus absolute e squared, times 256 minus 2 absolute e, all over 256 to the sixth.)

The experiment uses the 115 008-byte stream of all 1797 digit images, which
is longer than \(p\). It applies 300 corruptions of each kind and checks
the whole stream each time.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::string stream;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < digits.nrows(); ++r)
    for (int k = 0; k < 64; ++k) stream += static_cast<char>(digits.double_at("pixel_" + std::to_string(k), r));
  const std::uint32_t adler0 = alg::adler32(stream), crc0 = alg::crc32(stream);

  dm::random::SplitMix64 rng(2026);
  auto uniform = [&](std::size_t n) { return static_cast<std::size_t>(rng.next_u64() % n); };
  auto byte = [&](std::size_t i) { return static_cast<int>(static_cast<unsigned char>(stream[i])); };

  // Corrupt with a pattern of deltas at the given offsets; e is drawn so every byte stays in 0..255.
  auto trial = [&](const std::vector<std::size_t>& offsets, const std::vector<int>& weights) {
    while (true) {
      const std::size_t start = uniform(stream.size() - offsets.back());
      std::vector<int> choices;
      for (int e = -255; e <= 255; ++e)
        if (e != 0) {
          bool ok = true;
          for (std::size_t t = 0; t < offsets.size(); ++t) {
            const int y = byte(start + offsets[t]) + weights[t] * e;
            ok = ok && y >= 0 && y <= 255;
          }
          if (ok) choices.push_back(e);
        }
      if (choices.empty()) continue;
      const int e = choices[uniform(choices.size())];
      std::string copy = stream;
      for (std::size_t t = 0; t < offsets.size(); ++t)
        copy[start + offsets[t]] = static_cast<char>(byte(start + offsets[t]) + weights[t] * e);
      return std::pair{alg::adler32(copy) != adler0, alg::crc32(copy) != crc0};
    }
  };

  struct Pattern { std::string name; std::vector<std::size_t> offsets; std::vector<int> weights; };
  const std::vector<Pattern> patterns{
      {"one byte, +e", {0}, {1}},
      {"two bytes 1 apart, (+e, -e)", {0, 1}, {1, -1}},
      {"two bytes 1000 apart, (+e, -e)", {0, 1000}, {1, -1}},
      {"two bytes 65520 apart, (+e, -e)", {0, 65520}, {1, -1}},
      {"two bytes 65521 apart, (+e, -e)", {0, 65521}, {1, -1}},
      {"two bytes 65522 apart, (+e, -e)", {0, 65522}, {1, -1}},
      {"two bytes 65521 apart, (+e, +e)", {0, 65521}, {1, 1}},
      {"three adjacent bytes, (+e, -2e, +e)", {0, 1, 2}, {1, -2, 1}},
      {"three adjacent bytes, (+e, -e, +e)", {0, 1, 2}, {1, -1, 1}},
  };
  const int trials = 300;
  std::print("Stream: {} bytes, {} corruptions per pattern\n\n", stream.size(), trials);
  std::print("| corruption | Adler-32 detects | CRC-32 detects |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& pattern : patterns) {
    int adler = 0, crc = 0;
    for (int t = 0; t < trials; ++t) {
      const auto [a, c] = trial(pattern.offsets, pattern.weights);
      adler += a;
      crc += c;
    }
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", pattern.name, adler, crc);
  }

  double p3 = 0.0;
  for (int e = 1; e <= 127; ++e) p3 += 2.0 * (256.0 - e) * (256.0 - e) * (256.0 - 2.0 * e);
  p3 /= std::pow(256.0, 6);
  std::print("\nRandom 3-byte replacement: exact P3 = {:.3e}; Gaussian model {:.3e}\n", p3,
             alg::adler32_collision_probability(3));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Stream: 115008 bytes, 300 corruptions per pattern

| corruption | Adler-32 detects | CRC-32 detects |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| one byte, +e | 300 | 300 |
| two bytes 1 apart, (+e, -e) | 300 | 300 |
| two bytes 1000 apart, (+e, -e) | 300 | 300 |
| two bytes 65520 apart, (+e, -e) | 300 | 300 |
| two bytes 65521 apart, (+e, -e) | 0 | 300 |
| two bytes 65522 apart, (+e, -e) | 300 | 300 |
| two bytes 65521 apart, (+e, +e) | 300 | 300 |
| three adjacent bytes, (+e, -2e, +e) | 0 | 300 |
| three adjacent bytes, (+e, -e, +e) | 300 | 300 |

Random 3-byte replacement: exact P3 = 5.345e-06; Gaussian model 5.949e-06
// result_end
\caption(Detection of structured corruptions of the digits stream. Adler-32 misses exactly the patterns the algebra predicts; CRC-32 misses none)

Each prediction holds:

- Adler-32 misses every \((+e,-e)\) pair exactly 65521 bytes apart, and none
  at 65520 or 65522.
- It misses every \((e,-2e,e)\) burst.
- It catches the near misses, \((+e,+e)\) and \((e,-e,e)\), every time.
- CRC-32 catches all of them.

A random three-byte change goes unnoticed with probability
\(P_3=5.3\times10^{-6}\), about \(2^{-17.5}\). The Gaussian model below
puts it 11% higher, because the difference of two uniform bytes has a
triangular distribution, which is not yet close to normal at \(n=3\).

> How often do short messages collide?

The worst case above needs a particular pattern. A more common question is
how often two *unrelated* messages of the same length get the same
checksum. Treat the bytes as independent with variance \(v\). The closed
form makes \((A,B)\) a sum of independent terms, so it is approximately
normal, with

$$
\sigma_A^2 = nv,\qquad \sigma_B^2 = v\sum_{k=1}^n k^2 = v\,\frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6},\qquad
\operatorname{cov}(A,B) = v\sum_{k=1}^n k = v\,\frac{n(n+1)}{2} .
$$
\alttext(Sigma A squared equals n v; sigma B squared equals v times n times n plus 1 times 2n plus 1 over 6; the covariance of A and B equals v times n times n plus 1 over 2.)

The correlation tends to \(\sqrt3/2\), so the two sums are far from
independent. For two independent messages the difference
\((\Delta A,\Delta B)\) has covariance \(2\Sigma\). The two collide when
both differences are multiples of \(p\). With \(\Phi_p(s^2)\) for the
probability that a normal integer with variance \(s^2\) is a multiple of
\(p\) (a wrapped normal), the probability is

$$
P_n \approx \Phi_p\!\bigl(2\sigma_A^2\bigr)\;\Phi_p\!\bigl(2\sigma^2_{B\mid A}\bigr),\qquad
\sigma^2_{B\mid A}=\sigma_B^2-\frac{\operatorname{cov}(A,B)^2}{\sigma_A^2}=v\,\frac{n(n^2-1)}{12} .
$$
\alttext(P sub n is approximately Phi sub p of 2 sigma A squared times Phi sub p of 2 sigma B given A squared, where the conditional variance equals v n times n squared minus 1, over 12.)

\(\Phi_p(s^2)\) is \(1/\sqrt{2\pi s^2}\) while \(s\ll p\), and tends to
\(1/p\) once \(s\gg p\). This gives three regimes:

$$
P_n \approx \frac{\sqrt3}{2\pi v\,n\sqrt{n^2-1}} \;\;(n \lesssim 170), \qquad
P_n \approx \frac{1}{2p\sqrt{\pi n v}} \;\;(170 \lesssim n \lesssim 10^5), \qquad
P_n \to \frac1{p^2}.
$$
\alttext(For n below about 170, P sub n is about root 3 over 2 pi v n root of n squared minus 1. Between 170 and 100 thousand, P sub n is about 1 over 2 p root of pi n v. For longer messages it tends to 1 over p squared.)

The first regime ends when \(\Delta B\) given \(\Delta A\) spreads past
\(p\): with uniform bytes (\(v=5461\)) that is \(n^3\approx 6p^2/v\), or
\(n\approx170\). The second ends when \(\Delta A\) also wraps. By Poisson summation,
\(\Phi_p(s^2)=p^{-1}\bigl(1+2\sum_{m\ge1}e^{-2\pi^2m^2s^2/p^2}\bigr)\). With
\(s^2=2nv\), this is within 1% of \(1/p\) once
\(n\ge p^2\ln 200/(4\pi^2v)\approx1.06\times10^5\). An ideal 32-bit checksum has
\(2^{-32}\) at every length. The model, `algorithms::adler32_collision_probability`,
is not valid for \(n\le2\). There Adler-32 is one-to-one: with two bytes,
\(x_1=B-A-1\) and \(x_2=A-1-x_1\).

To test it, draw \(N=2^{18}\) random messages per length. The expected
number of colliding pairs is \(\binom N2P_n\), where
\(\binom N2\approx3.4\times10^{10}\). Pairs of identical messages, which
only occur at \(n\le 4\), are excluded. For CRC-32 the expectation is
\(3.4\times10^{10}/2^{32}=8\) pairs at every length.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/adler32_collisions.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

// Number of pairs with equal keys in a sorted vector.
template <class T> double equal_pairs(std::vector<T> keys) {
  std::ranges::sort(keys);
  double pairs = 0;
  for (std::size_t i = 0, j; i < keys.size(); i = j) {
    for (j = i; j < keys.size() && keys[j] == keys[i]; ++j) {}
    pairs += (j - i) * (j - i - 1) / 2.0;
  }
  return pairs;
}

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::size_t N = 1 << 18;
  const double pairs = N * (N - 1.0) / 2.0, ideal = std::ldexp(1.0, -32);
  dm::random::SplitMix64 rng(42);

  std::vector<double> log_n, log_adler, log_crc;
  std::print("| n | model P | expected pairs | Adler-32 pairs | CRC-32 pairs |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t n : {3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32, 48, 64, 96, 128, 192, 256, 512, 1024, 2048, 4096}) {
    std::vector<std::uint32_t> adler, crc;
    std::vector<std::pair<std::uint32_t, std::uint64_t>> same;  // (checksum, message) for short messages
    std::string message(n, '\0');
    for (std::size_t m = 0; m < N; ++m) {
      for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; i += 8) {
        const std::uint64_t r = rng.next_u64();
        for (std::size_t b = 0; b < 8 && i + b < n; ++b) message[i + b] = static_cast<char>(r >> (8 * b));
      }
      adler.push_back(alg::adler32(message));
      crc.push_back(alg::crc32(message));
      if (n <= 8) {
        std::uint64_t key = 0;
        std::memcpy(&key, message.data(), n);
        same.emplace_back(adler.back(), key);
      }
    }
    const double identical = n <= 8 ? equal_pairs(same) : 0.0;
    const double a = equal_pairs(adler) - identical, c = equal_pairs(crc) - identical;
    const double model = alg::adler32_collision_probability(n);
    std::print("| {} | {:.2e} | {:.1f} | {} | {} |\n", n, model, model * pairs, a, c);
    log_n.push_back(std::log10(n));
    log_adler.push_back(a > 0 ? std::log10(a / pairs) : std::nan(""));
    log_crc.push_back(c > 0 ? std::log10(c / pairs) : std::nan(""));
  }

  std::vector<double> grid_x, grid_model;
  for (std::uint64_t n = 3; n <= 10'000'000; n = std::max(n + 1, n * 21 / 20)) {
    grid_x.push_back(std::log10(n));
    grid_model.push_back(std::log10(alg::adler32_collision_probability(n)));
  }
  std::vector<double> ax, ay, cx, cy;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < log_n.size(); ++i) {
    if (!std::isnan(log_adler[i])) ax.push_back(log_n[i]), ay.push_back(log_adler[i]);
    if (!std::isnan(log_crc[i])) cx.push_back(log_n[i]), cy.push_back(log_crc[i]);
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(grid_x, grid_model, "Adler-32 model", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
      .points(ax, ay, "Adler-32 simulated", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 3.5)
      .points(cx, cy, "CRC-32 simulated", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 3.5)
      .lines({grid_x.front(), 7.0}, {std::log10(ideal), std::log10(ideal)}, "ideal 2^-32", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.0)
      .x_limits(0.4, 7.0)
      .y_limits(-11, -4.5)
      .title("Probability that two random n-byte messages collide")
      .x_label("log10 message length n (bytes)")
      .y_label("log10 collision probability")
      .size(860, 540)
      .save_svg("build/adler32_collisions.svg");
}
```
