\image(figures/adler32_collisions.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Colliding pairs among \(2^{18}\) random messages per length, the model's expectation against the observed counts, and the same on log-log axes. The model line bends from slope \(-2\) to slope \(-1/2\) between 100 and 200 bytes and reaches \(2^{-32}\) only beyond \(10^5\) bytes; CRC-32 sits on \(2^{-32}\) throughout)
\alttext(A log-log chart. The blue model line falls steeply from about 10 to the minus 5 at 3 bytes, bends between 100 and 200 bytes, then falls slowly and meets the black horizontal line at 2 to the minus 32 near 100 thousand bytes. Blue simulated points lie on the line. Red CRC points scatter around the black line.)

The model matches the simulation:

- **Short messages.** At \(n=3\), 183 358 colliding pairs give
  \(5.34\times10^{-6}\), against the exact \(P_3=5.35\times10^{-6}\). The
  Gaussian model is 11% high at \(n=3\), 4% at \(n=8\), and within 3%
  from \(n=16\) on.
- **Long messages.** Counts agree within Poisson noise.
- **Slopes.** From 8 to 64 bytes the observed slope is \(-2.0\); from 256
  to 4096 bytes it is \(-0.50\), as derived.

At 64 bytes, the size of a small network packet, Adler-32 collides 50
times more often than an ideal 32-bit check. That is why SCTP replaced it
with CRC-32c (RFC 3309).

CRC-32 has *no* collisions at 3 and 4 bytes. CRC is linear and invertible
on 32-bit inputs, so it is one-to-one there. At longer lengths its counts
scatter around 8; a check over 24 seeds at 16 bytes averaged 7.4.

> Real data: handwritten digits

Random bytes are the most favourable case. Real data have low entropy and
strong structure. Each of the 1797 images in `dm::datasets::digits()` is
64 bytes of ink intensity from 0 to 16, read in row-major order. For these
images the two sums have a meaning:

- \(A-1\) is the total ink.
- \((B-64)/(A-1) = 65-\bar\imath\), where \(\bar\imath\) is the
  ink-weighted mean pixel index, so it measures how high the ink sits.

The values are small (\(B<p\) always), so nothing wraps. The checksum
therefore reduces to two image features, and similar digits have similar
features.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::vector<std::string> images;
  std::vector<int> label;
  double sum = 0, sum2 = 0;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < digits.nrows(); ++r) {
    std::string image;
    for (int k = 0; k < 64; ++k) {
      const double x = digits.double_at("pixel_" + std::to_string(k), r);
      image += static_cast<char>(x);
      sum += x;
      sum2 += x * x;
    }
    images.push_back(image);
    label.push_back(static_cast<int>(digits.double_at("digit", r)));
  }
  const double cells = 64.0 * images.size(), mean = sum / cells, v = sum2 / cells - mean * mean;

  std::map<std::uint32_t, std::vector<std::size_t>> by_adler;
  std::set<std::uint32_t> crcs;
  std::set<std::string> distinct(images.begin(), images.end());
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < images.size(); ++i) {
    by_adler[alg::adler32(images[i])].push_back(i);
    crcs.insert(alg::crc32(images[i]));
  }
  int colliding_pairs = 0, same_digit = 0;
  std::string examples;
  for (const auto& [checksum, rows] : by_adler)
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < rows.size(); ++i)
      for (std::size_t j = i + 1; j < rows.size(); ++j) {
        ++colliding_pairs;
        same_digit += label[rows[i]] == label[rows[j]];
        examples += std::format("| {} and {} | {} and {} | 0x{:08X} |\n", rows[i], rows[j], label[rows[i]],
                                label[rows[j]], checksum);
      }
  const double n_img = distinct.size();
  std::print("| quantity | value |\n| :--- | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| images (all distinct) | {} |\n", distinct.size());
  std::print("| byte mean, variance | {:.2f}, {:.2f} |\n", mean, v);
  std::print("| distinct CRC-32 values | {} |\n", crcs.size());
  std::print("| distinct Adler-32 values | {} |\n", by_adler.size());
  std::print("| colliding Adler-32 pairs | {} |\n", colliding_pairs);
  std::print("| model expectation, iid bytes with this variance | {:.2f} |\n",
             n_img * (n_img - 1) / 2 * alg::adler32_collision_probability(64, v));
  std::print("| model expectation, uniform bytes | {:.5f} |\n",
             n_img * (n_img - 1) / 2 * alg::adler32_collision_probability(64));
  std::print("| colliding pairs showing the same digit | {} |\n\n", same_digit);
  std::print("| rows | digits | shared Adler-32 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n{}", examples);
}
```
