| distinct Adler-32 values | 1788 |
| colliding Adler-32 pairs | 9 |
| model expectation, iid bytes with this variance | 3.00 |
| model expectation, uniform bytes | 0.01989 |
| colliding pairs showing the same digit | 0 |

| rows | digits | shared Adler-32 |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 130 and 570 | 0 and 2 | 0x21DF0116 |
| 957 and 1616 | 0 and 9 | 0x220F0117 |
| 115 and 304 | 2 and 0 | 0x2351011A |
| 366 and 1102 | 4 and 2 | 0x23E00137 |
| 466 and 1450 | 1 and 5 | 0x27CF0131 |
| 616 and 1446 | 4 and 9 | 0x29E90147 |
| 292 and 1738 | 0 and 5 | 0x2A5E0132 |
| 431 and 1028 | 3 and 8 | 0x2AA40156 |
| 52 and 96 | 7 and 8 | 0x31360163 |
// result_end
\caption(Adler-32 and CRC-32 over the 1797 digit images, against the collision model)

Two different images share a checksum nine times, three times the
expectation for independent bytes with the same variance. Every colliding
pair shows two *different* digits. CRC-32 separates all 1797 images.

The scatter plots the two sums for each image, coloured by digit. All the
points lie in a band covering about \(250\times8000\) of the
\(65521\times65521\) possible checksums, a fraction of about \(5\times10^{-4}\).
\(B\) weights the top of the image most, so digits with ink at the top
(7) lie along the upper edge of the band. Digits with ink at the bottom (6)
lie along the lower edge. The 1s reach both ends of the ink range, thin
strokes at the low end and heavy ones at the high end. Elsewhere the
digits overlap, and that is where the collisions between different digits
come from.

```{cpp, results="output", file="figures/adler32_digit_sums.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> a(10), b(10);
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < digits.nrows(); ++r) {
    std::string image;
    for (int k = 0; k < 64; ++k) image += static_cast<char>(digits.double_at("pixel_" + std::to_string(k), r));
    const std::uint32_t sum = alg::adler32(image);
    const int d = static_cast<int>(digits.double_at("digit", r));
    a[d].push_back(sum & 0xFFFF);
    b[d].push_back(sum >> 16);
  }
  const std::vector<dm::RGB> colors{{31, 119, 180}, {255, 127, 14}, {44, 160, 44}, {214, 39, 40}, {148, 103, 189},
                                    {140, 86, 75},  {227, 119, 194}, {127, 127, 127}, {188, 189, 34}, {23, 190, 207}};
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  for (int d = 0; d < 10; ++d) plot.points(a[d], b[d], std::to_string(d), colors[d], 2.0);
  plot.x_limits(170, 520)
      .title("Adler-32 halves of the 1797 digit images")
      .x_label("A = 1 + total ink")
      .y_label("B = 64 + position-weighted ink")
      .size(860, 560)
      .save_svg("build/adler32_digit_sums.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/adler32_digit_sums.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The two Adler-32 sums of every digit image: total ink and its vertical position. 7s lie along the top of the band, 6s along the bottom, and 1s at both ends of the ink range)
\alttext(A scatter plot with A from about 185 to 440 on the x axis and B from about 6 thousand to 14 thousand on the y axis. The points lie in a band rising from lower left to upper right. Orange ones sit at both ends of the ink range, grey sevens along the upper edge, pink sixes along the lower edge, and the other digits overlap in the middle.)

> Summary

- Adler-32 is \(A=1+\sum x_i\) and \(B=n+\sum(n-i+1)x_i\), modulo 65521. Linearity gives constant-time combining and rolling, and the bound \(255n(n+1)/2+(n+1)(p-1)<2^{32}\) gives zlib's 5552-byte run.
- It catches every one-byte error. It misses two-byte errors only at distances that are multiples of 65521. It always misses three-byte patterns \((e,-2e,e)\), such as "aca" and "bab", which CRC-32 never misses.
- For two random messages the collision probability falls as \(n^{-2}\) up to about 170 bytes, then as \(n^{-1/2}\). It is within 1% of \(2^{-32}\) only after about \(10^5\) bytes. Simulation agrees with this model across three orders of magnitude.
- On the digit images, the two sums act as features (ink and its position), and collisions are more frequent than for random bytes of the same variance. For short or structured records, prefer CRC-32. Adler-32 suits what zlib uses it for: long, compressed streams.

> References

- P. Deutsch, J.-L. Gailly (1996). *ZLIB Compressed Data Format Specification version 3.3*, RFC 1950, section 8.2.
- J. Stone, M. Stewart, D. Otis (2002). *Stream Control Transmission Protocol (SCTP) Checksum Change*, RFC 3309. Explains why SCTP dropped Adler-32 for short packets.
- T. C. Maxino, P. J. Koopman (2009). The effectiveness of checksums for embedded control networks. *IEEE Transactions on Dependable and Secure Computing* 6(1), 59\-\-72.
- A. Tridgell, P. Mackerras (1996). *The rsync algorithm*. Technical report TR-CS-96-05, Australian National University.
